1. Introduction
1.1 Overview
JPetStore is a web-based e-commerce application developed around the concept of an online pet store. The application allows customers to browse various categories of pets and pet-related products, view detailed product information, maintain shopping carts, and place orders through a user-friendly interface. The project simulates the functionality of a real-world e-commerce platform and provides an understanding of the various components involved in enterprise application development.
The application follows a layered architecture that separates presentation, business logic, data access, and database functionalities. Such an architectural approach improves maintainability, scalability, and reusability by ensuring that each layer has a clearly defined responsibility. The system is designed to demonstrate industry-standard software development practices and support end-to-end business processes starting from product browsing and cart management to order placement and order history tracking.
The official JPetStore 6 implementation is developed using Java as the primary programming language and leverages Spring Framework for dependency management and application services. The web layer is implemented using the Stripes MVC framework, while database interaction is handled through MyBatis. Persistent data storage is provided by HSQLDB, a lightweight relational database management system. Together, these technologies create a complete enterprise application environment suitable for learning modern software engineering concepts.
The major business functionalities supported by the application include user authentication, category management, product catalogue management, shopping cart operations, order creation, database management, and testing activities.
1.2 Purpose of the Document
The purpose of this Planning and Design Document is to describe the overall design, architecture, implementation approach, and project organization for the JPetStore application. The document provides a comprehensive overview of the system and serves as a reference for all stakeholders involved in the project lifecycle, including developers, testers, reviewers, and project coordinators.
This document outlines the functional requirements, architectural design, high-level and low-level system design, technology stack, project scope, team responsibilities, testing approach, and implementation timeline. 
It also provides sufficient information to ensure consistency in development activities and acts as a baseline for future enhancements, maintenance activities, and project reviews.
By documenting the technical and functional aspects of the application, the project team can ensure a common understanding of project objectives and implementation strategies throughout the development process.
1.3 Objectives
The Presentation Layer provides the front-end interface through which users interact with the application. It contains pages for registration, login, category browsing, product viewing, cart management, and order placement.
Technology Used: HTML, CSS, JavaScript
Responsibilities:
•	Provide user interfaces
•	Capture user inputs
•	Display products, cart details, and order information
•	Send HTTP requests to backend REST endpoints
•	Display HTTP responses received from the backend 
The primary objective of the JPetStore project is to develop a fully functional online pet-store application that accurately represents the workflow of a typical e-commerce system. The project aims to provide practical experience in building multi-layered enterprise applications using Java-based technologies and established architectural principles.
Another objective is to implement a modular and maintainable solution that separates presentation concerns from business logic and database operations. The application should provide secure user authentication, efficient product browsing facilities, shopping cart management capabilities, and order-processing workflows.
The project also aims to demonstrate effective integration between user interface components, application services, persistence mechanisms, and relational databases. In addition, functional testing and performance validation activities will be carried out to ensure system reliability and quality. Throughout development, industry best practices such as code modularity, maintainability, and reusability will be followed to ensure the long-term sustainability of the application.

 
2. Architecture Design
2.1 Architecture Overview
The JPetStore application follows a layered architecture that separates the presentation, backend application, data access, and database responsibilities. This separation improves maintainability, reusability, and communication between different components. The architecture and request flow are illustrated in Figure 2.1
The major layers are:
Presentation Layer
The Presentation Layer provides the user interface through which users interact with the JPetStore application. It is responsible for displaying information to users and collecting user inputs for various business operations. This layer includes web pages for user registration, login, category browsing, product viewing, shopping cart management, and order placement. It ensures a user-friendly experience and acts as the communication point between users and the backend application.
Technologies Used: HTML, CSS, JavaScript
Responsibilities:
•	Display user interfaces
•	Capture user inputs
•	Present product and order information
•	Send requests to the backend system
•	Display responses received from the application
Backend Application Layer
The Backend Application Layer receives requests from the frontend and processes them through the REST API and Spring MVC Controllers. The controllers validate request details and delegate processing to the appropriate service components.
Technologies Used: Java, Spring Boot, Spring MVC
Responsibilities:
•	Expose REST API endpoints
•	Receive HTTP requests containing JSON data
•	Validate request parameters
•	Route requests to appropriate services
•	Handle application-level responses
•	Coordinate communication between frontend and business services




Business Layer

The Business Layer contains the core business logic of the application. Services process user, category, product, cart, and order operations. The layer performs business validations before requesting data from, or sending data to, the data-access layer.
Technologies Used: Java, Spring Framework
Responsibilities:
•	Implement application business rules
•	Process user authentication
•	Manage category and catalogue operations
•	Validate product availability
•	Handle adding, updating, and removing cart items
•	Calculate cart and order totals
•	Create and process orders
•	Coordinate related data operations

Data Access Layer

The Data Access Layer provides communication between the business layer and the MySQL database. Repository classes perform CRUD operations and convert database records into entity or model objects used by the application.
Technology Used: Repository Classes with JDBC or the configured database-access framework
Responsibilities:
•	Execute database operations
•	Retrieve application records
•	Insert, update, and delete records
•	Map database results to entity/model objects
•	Provide a consistent interface for database communication
•	Maintain the separation between business logic and SQL operations








Database Layer

The Database Layer provides persistent storage for application data. MySQL stores user details, categories, products, cart information, order records, and order-item details.
Technology Used: MySQL
Supporting Tool: MySQL Workbench
Responsibilities:
•	Store application data
•	Maintain relational tables and relationships
•	Enforce primary-key and foreign-key constraints
•	Support database queries and updates
•	Maintain data consistency and integrity
•	Provide persistent storage for business transactions

 
